  "text": "Both solutions answer N, and I don't think that is correct for general N.\n\nSolution 1 reasons that each of the N solitons contributes one pole, but it doesn't justify why each soliton's pole pair splits as one in the upper half-plane. Solution 2 has the same gap: it treats the N-soliton solution as if it had one upper-half-plane pole per soliton, and it ignores the interaction poles of the Wronskian construction. Its own assumption that there are no \"additional interaction poles\" is asserted, not shown.\n\nI recall the result from the literature on rational solutions of the massive Thirring model (Pelinovsky and Shimabukuro's work on the double-Wronskian hierarchy). As I remember it, the N-th rational solution has N(N+1)/2 poles in the upper half-plane for large |t|, under the stated assumption that the leading-order polynomials have N real roots. This is a triangular number, and it matches N = 1, where a single algebraic soliton has one upper-half-plane pole. For N = 2 it gives 3, not 2. I could not verify this against the source in this session, since I have no search tools loaded, so treat it as a recollection rather than a checked fact.\n\nSince both proposed answers give N, and N(N+1)/2 differs from N for every N ≥ 2, neither solution has the correct final answer.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
